 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "This further study compares one centralised and one egalitarian society, for example the Bamum kingdom and the Beti.\nBamum (centralised): capital Foumban; ruled by a sultan (mfon) from a large palace; officials and secret societies; an army; tribute; King Njoya (ruled about 1886–1933) invented a script, a religion and a map of his kingdom and built a new palace.\nBeti (egalitarian): in the Centre and South; independent villages; authority of family heads (nkukuma, rich and respected men) and councils of elders; the so ceremony (initiation) and other rites united them; no central army or taxes.\nTo compare, use headings: political organisation, economy, religion, culture and achievements.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
